tial reduction of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ centers (Figure 4f). Fitting of the $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ peak with $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ components converged at 1:3 ratio, with binding energy of $458.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 458.5 $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{eV}}$ , respectively. The two weak signals at $461.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 466.9 $\mathrm{eV}$ are likely $\dot{\mathrm{Ti}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ shake-up satellites, which are characteristic of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers with unpaired d electrons.38-39
The XANES pre-edge region of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}{}_{2}\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{IV}}{}_{6}$ -BDC-CoH showed an overall $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ shift to lower energy compared to that of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{IV}}{}_{8}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC-CoCl}$ , due to the presence of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{IIf}}$ centers which enables the lower energy $1\mathrm{s}\mathrm{\longrightarrow}\hat{3}\mathrm{d}^{1}$ transition in addition to the $1\mathrm{s}{\longrightarrow}3\mathrm{d}^{0}$ transition (Figure 4e).39 Furthermore, the preedge region of $\mathrm{Ti_{8}\mathrm{-}B D C\mathrm{-}C o H}$ has higher overall intensity than that of the $\operatorname{Ti}_{8}$ -BDC-CoCl, presumably due to geometry distortion of $\left[\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\right]^{3-}$ or $\left[\mathrm{TiO}_{6}\right]^{2-}$ octahedra caused by partial reduction, which leads to higher probability of spin-forbidden 1s to 3d transition.40 In contrast, $\mathrm{\dot{T}i^{I V}}_{8}$ -BTC-CoH displays identical pre-edge signals as $\operatorname{Ti}_{8}$ -BDC-CoCl, supporting the assignment of the aforementioned features to a change in the Ti oxidation state.
